ROWS = [
dict(id="T177", d="TREE", t="The critical regime of the Derrida–Retaux recursive model",
 s="Fix an integer m ≥ 2 and a Z_+-valued random variable X_0, and define X_{n+1} = (X_n^{(1)} + … + X_n^{(m)} − 1)^+, where X_n^{(1)}, …, X_n^{(m)} are independent copies of X_n. Assume the critical condition (m − 1)E(X_0 m^{X_0}) = E(m^{X_0}) < ∞. Chen, Derrida, Hu, Lifshits and Shi conjecture that P(X_n ≠ 0) ~ (4/(m − 1)²) n^{−2} as n → ∞, and that conditionally on X_n ≠ 0 the law of X_n converges to the geometric law P(Y = k) = (m − 1)/m^k, k ≥ 1; consequently E(X_n) ~ (4m/(m − 1)³) n^{−2}.",
 c="The recursion describes a hierarchical renormalisation model for the depinning transition with strong disorder, and it appears earlier as a spin-glass toy model of Collet, Eckmann, Glaser and Martin, who identified the critical condition. A separate conjecture of Derrida and Retaux concerns the near-critical free energy: for X_0 with law (1 − p)δ_0 + pP_{Y_0} and p_c > 0, F_∞(p) = exp(−(K + o(1))(p − p_c)^{−1/2}) as p ↓ p_c, an infinite-order phase transition. The present row is the critical-point conjecture, not the free-energy one.",
 src="X. Chen, B. Derrida, Y. Hu, M. Lifshits and Z. Shi, A max-type recursive model: some properties and open questions, in Sojourns in Probability Theory and Statistical Physics III, Springer Proc. Math. Stat. 300 (2019) 166–186; B. Derrida and M. Retaux, J. Stat. Phys. 156 (2014) 268–290",
 loc="Chen–Derrida–Hu–Lifshits–Shi, arXiv:1705.04787v2, Section 1, Theorem 2: 'Assume (4). Then lim_{n→∞} E(m^{X_n}) = 1.' Conjecture 1: 'Assume (4). Then we have P(X_n ≠ 0) ∼ 4/(m−1)² · 1/n², n → ∞.' and 'When m = 2, Conjecture 1 was already given by Collet et al. [3] (see their equation (AIII.10)) and by Derrida and Retaux [5].'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.04787",
 why="The Derrida–Retaux recursion is the simplest model believed to sit in the universality class of hierarchical pinning with relevant disorder, and the critical asymptotics are the quantitative signature of that class. The n^{−2} law and the geometric conditional limit are predicted by physicists and have been the target of a sequence of probabilistic papers since 2017.",
 st="Chen, Hu and Shi (arXiv:2108.05610) proved P(X_n > 0) = n^{−2+o(1)} at criticality, described by the authors as a weaker confirmation of the predictions. Chen, Dagard, Derrida, Hu, Lifshits and Shi (Ann. Probab. 49 (2021) 637–670, arXiv:1907.01601) confirmed the free-energy conjecture under an integrability condition, namely F_∞(p) = exp(−(C + o(1))(p − p_c)^{−1/2}) as p ↓ p_c; Chen (arXiv:2405.09741) proves the free energy is infinitely differentiable at p_c. Exactly solvable relatives are treated by Hu, Mallein and Pain (arXiv:1811.08749, continuous time on a Yule tree) and by Alsmeyer, Hu and Mallein (arXiv:2502.02991, geometric Galton–Watson tree); the dual subcritical regime is in arXiv:2306.12717; Duquesne and Shi (arXiv:2609.11435) study the continuous Derrida–Retaux branching process, conjectured to be the scaling limit of the discrete model. The sharp asymptotics of Conjectures 1 and 2 remain unproved. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): central in the probabilistic study of hierarchical renormalisation models and well known to specialists in branching and recursive distributional equations; less known outside that community."),
dict(id="T178", d="RW", t="Recurrence and transience of rotor walk on Z^d with i.i.d. uniform initial rotors",
 s="On Z^d each vertex x carries a rotor ρ(x) ∈ {±e_1, …, ±e_d}, and the rotors advance through the 2d directions in a fixed cyclic order. A particle at x first advances the rotor at x to the next direction and then steps to x + ρ(x). Choose the initial rotors independently and uniformly at random. Is the resulting rotor walk started at the origin recurrent (returns to the origin infinitely often) almost surely in dimension d = 2, and transient almost surely in dimensions d ≥ 3?",
 c="By Angel and Holroyd, two initial configurations differing in finitely many rotors are both recurrent or both transient, so recurrence of the i.i.d. configuration is a tail event and has probability 0 or 1. Schramm showed that rotor walk is at least as recurrent as simple random walk for every initial configuration, so the question is the transience half in d ≥ 3 and the recurrence half in d = 2 for this particular random environment. The deterministic aligned configuration in d ≥ 3 is transient (Florescu–Ganguly–Levine–Peres). Compare T101 (the Lorentz mirror model), whose connection to rotor walks with random initial rotors is used in the Manhattan-lattice result below.",
 src="L. Florescu, S. Ganguly, L. Levine and Y. Peres, Escape rates for rotor walk in Z^d, SIAM J. Discrete Math. 28 (2014) 323–334; the recurrence question for rotor walk goes back to J. Propp's 2003 lecture",
 loc="Florescu–Ganguly–Levine–Peres, arXiv:1301.3521v2, 'Some open questions': 'Let us choose initial rotors ρ(x) for x ∈ Z^d independently and uniformly at random from {±e1, . . . , ±ed}. Is the resulting rotor walk recurrent in dimension 2 and transient in dimensions d ≥ 3?' followed by 'Hence the set of recurrent ρ is a tail event and consequently has probability 0 or 1.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.3521",
 why="Rotor walk is the standard deterministic analogue of random walk, and the i.i.d. uniform configuration is the most natural random environment for it, sitting between random walk in random environment and self-organised models such as the abelian sandpile. Whether the Pólya dichotomy survives derandomisation in this setting has been asked since Propp's lecture and is unresolved in every dimension.",
 st="On regular trees Angel and Holroyd showed that the i.i.d. uniform configuration has escape rate 1/b on the b-ary tree for b ≥ 3 and is recurrent for b = 2. Florescu, Levine and Peres (arXiv:1408.5533) proved that the range of any rotor walk on a periodic Eulerian graph in R^d is at least of order t^{d/(d+1)}, and used the mirror model and critical percolation to prove recurrence with i.i.d. uniform rotors on the Manhattan lattice and the F-lattice. Kaiser and Sava-Huss (arXiv:2210.00810) proved recurrence on the Sierpinski gasket graph; Huss and Sava-Huss (arXiv:1805.05746, 1805.11983) studied range and speed on trees. Bou-Rabee and Peres (arXiv:2608.23545) settle d = 2: with i.i.d. uniform initial rotors the walker visits every site of Z^2 infinitely often, so the walk is recurrent, and the visited region at time t, rescaled by t^{1/3}, converges to a convex body, as Priezzhev, Dhar, Dhar and Krishnamurthy predicted. The preprint is not yet refereed. Transience in dimensions d ≥ 3 is open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a well-known question in the rotor-router literature, posed by Propp and repeated by Peres and coauthors; known mainly within discrete probability."),
dict(id="T179", d="BM", t="Uniqueness in law for the stochastic heat equation with non-degenerate Hölder noise coefficient below exponent 2/3",
 s="On the one-dimensional torus T consider ∂_t u = Δu + g(u)Ẇ with Ẇ space-time white noise, where g is uniformly elliptic (|g| bounded away from 0) and globally β-Hölder continuous. Weak uniqueness (uniqueness in law) is known for β > 2/3. Does it hold for every β ∈ (0, 2/3], or at least for β > 1/2?",
 c="Without ellipticity uniqueness can fail: Mueller, Mytnik and Perkins constructed non-unique solutions for g(x) = |x|^γ with γ < 3/4, as described by Han, so the role of the condition |g| ≥ c > 0 is the whole question. Pathwise uniqueness for β > 3/4, without ellipticity, is due to Mytnik and Perkins. The finite-dimensional analogue (uniqueness in law for SDEs with uniformly elliptic continuous diffusion coefficient, Stroock–Varadhan) suggests that ellipticity alone might give uniqueness for every β > 0, and Han records that no counterexample is known for any β > 0.",
 src="Y. Han, Stochastic heat equation with nondegenerate Hölder diffusion coefficient: uniqueness below the three-fourth threshold, arXiv:2608.01279 (2026)",
 loc="Han, arXiv:2608.01279, Section 1.3.1: 'We conjecture that 2/3 is not the sharp threshold for weak uniqueness, as no counterexamples are known for non-degenerate g for any β > 0. The two natural thresholds beyond 2/3 are thus β > 1/2 and β > 0.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.01279",
 why="Uniqueness for stochastic PDEs with non-Lipschitz noise coefficients is one of the basic open themes in SPDE theory, motivated by super-Brownian motion and the Fleming–Viot process, where the coefficient is √u. The elliptic case isolates whether the infinite-dimensional problem behaves like the finite-dimensional theory of Stroock and Varadhan, where continuity and ellipticity suffice.",
 st="Mytnik and Perkins (Probab. Theory Related Fields 149 (2011) 1–96) proved pathwise uniqueness for β > 3/4. Mueller, Mytnik and Perkins (Ann. Probab. 42 (2014) 2032–2112) gave non-uniqueness, both in law and pathwise, for the non-elliptic coefficient |u|^γ with γ < 3/4. Bass and Perkins proved uniqueness for elliptic coefficients 1/2 + ε-Hölder in an enhanced norm that does not cover pointwise coefficients. Han's generalized coupling method gave weak uniqueness for elliptic g with β > 3/4, and Han (arXiv:2608.01279) lowered this to β > 2/3. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): the general question of uniqueness for SPDEs with Hölder coefficients is well known in stochastic analysis; this elliptic threshold form is recent.",
 x="SOLVED (claimed, unrefereed), 15 Nov 2026: Y. Han, Weak uniqueness for the stochastic heat equation with nondegenerate diffusion: the full Hölder regime (arXiv:2611.07310v1, Nov 2026). Abstract, read 15 Nov 2026: weak well-posedness on the one-dimensional torus for uniformly nondegenerate diffusion Hölder of any positive order, which 'resolves the weak uniqueness problem in the full positive Hölder range'. A round-3 Codex check on Mac mini 1 (06_math_research/codex_op_1114/round3/T179) extends Han's bounded-start uniqueness (Prop. 8.1, as cited there) to every deterministic L2 initial state through an L1 contraction of the coupled approximations; Han's proof itself was not audited."),
dict(id="T180", d="STAT", t="The optimal competitive ratio for the prophet secretary problem",
 s="Independent nonnegative random variables X_1, …, X_n with known distributions are revealed in a uniformly random order, and a gambler must accept or reject each value irrevocably on seeing it, receiving the accepted value. Determine the largest constant c such that for every n and every choice of distributions some online stopping rule achieves expected reward at least c · E[max_i X_i].",
 c="With adversarial order the optimal constant is 1/2 (Krengel–Sucheston, Garling); with i.i.d. values it is about 0.745 (Hill–Kertz, Correa–Foncea–Hoeksma–Oosterwijk–Vredeveld). The random-order constant lies between these. The related order-selection problem, where the gambler chooses the order, has its own optimal constant, also unknown; Bubna and Chiplunkar separated the two settings.",
 src="H. Esfandiari, M. Hajiaghayi, V. Liaghat and M. Monemizadeh, Prophet secretary, SIAM J. Discrete Math. 31 (2017) 1685–1701; status as in Z. Chen, Z. Huang, D. Li and Z. G. Tang, arXiv:2411.01191",
 loc="Chen–Huang–Li–Tang, arXiv:2411.01191, Section 1: 'The study of prophet secretary was initiated by Esfandiari et al. [16], who designed a 1−1/e ≈ 0.632 competitive algorithm and provided an upper bound of 0.75.' and 'The state-of-the-art lower and upper bounds are 0.672 by Harb [25] and 0.723 by Giambartolomei et al. [23] respectively.' Bubna–Chiplunkar, arXiv:2211.04145, abstract: 'the optimum ratio still remains unknown in the order selection setting, as well as in prophet secretary, where the items arrive in a random order.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.01191",
 why="Prophet inequalities are the basic comparison between online and offline optimal stopping, with applications to posted-price mechanisms. The random-order version is the natural intermediate between the classical adversarial bound 1/2 and the i.i.d. bound, and pinning down its constant has resisted a decade of work on both the algorithmic and the hardness side.",
 st="Esfandiari et al. obtained 1 − 1/e. Azar, Chiplunkar and Kaplan, and Correa, Saona and Ziliotto improved this, the latter showing that blind strategies cannot exceed 0.675. Harb (arXiv:2307.00971) obtained 0.672, and Chen, Huang, Li and Tang (arXiv:2411.01191) obtained 0.688 with a non-blind algorithm. On the hardness side Bubna and Chiplunkar (arXiv:2211.04145) showed 0.7254, and Giambartolomei et al. obtained 0.723, the hardness bound cited by Chen et al. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a well-known open problem in optimal stopping and algorithmic game theory; prominent in theoretical computer science, less so in classical probability."),
dict(id="T181", d="GEOM", t="Exact decay rate of the Favard length (Buffon needle probability) of the four-corner Cantor set",
 s="Let K_n be the n-th generation of the four-corner Cantor set: the union of the 4^n squares of side 4^{−n} obtained from the unit square by keeping the four corner squares of side 1/4 and iterating. Fav(K_n), the average over directions of the length of the orthogonal projection of K_n, is proportional to the probability that a randomly thrown long needle meets K_n. Determine the exact rate at which Fav(K_n) tends to 0.",
 c="Fav(K_n) → 0 by Besicovitch's projection theorem, since K is purely unrectifiable. A lower bound c/n follows from Mattila's capacity bound, improved to c log n/n by Bateman and Volberg (arXiv:0807.2953); Nazarov, Peres and Volberg (arXiv:0801.2942) proved an upper bound C n^{−p} with p below 1/6, and note that a random analogue has Fav comparable to 1/n while it is not clear whether the deterministic set decays at that rate. The exponent is thus known only to lie between 1/6 and 1, and the random comparison makes n^{−1} up to logarithmic factors the natural guess. Other self-similar sets (rational product Cantor sets, the Sierpinski gasket) raise the same question.",
 src="F. Nazarov, Y. Peres and A. Volberg, The power law for the Buffon needle probability of the four-corner Cantor set, Algebra i Analiz 22 (2010) 82–97; English transl. St. Petersburg Math. J. 22 (2011)",
 loc="Nazarov–Peres–Volberg, arXiv:0801.2942, abstract: 'A classical theorem of Besicovitch implies that the Favard length of K_n tends to zero. It is still an open problem to determine its exact rate of decay.' Introduction: 'A lower bound Fav(K_n) ≥ c/n for some c > 0 follows from Mattila [8, 1.4].' and, after Theorem 1, 'a bound decaying faster than O(n^{−1/4}) would require new ideas.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/0801.2942",
 why="The four-corner Cantor set is the canonical purely unrectifiable set, and the decay of its Favard length is the model problem for making Besicovitch's projection theorem quantitative. The question links harmonic analysis (zeros of trigonometric mask polynomials), additive combinatorics and geometric measure theory, and it is closely tied to analytic capacity and Vitushkin's conjecture.",
 st="Peres and Solomyak proved Fav(K_n) ≤ C exp(−a log* n). Nazarov, Peres and Volberg (arXiv:0801.2942) proved Fav(K_n) ≤ C n^{−1/6+δ}. Bateman and Volberg improved the lower bound to c log n / n (Math. Res. Lett. 17 (2010) 959–967). Łaba and Zhai (arXiv:0902.0964), Bond, Łaba and Volberg (arXiv:1109.1031) and Łaba and Marshall (arXiv:2202.07555) extended power-law bounds to rational product Cantor sets, and Marshall (arXiv:2509.02882) obtained N^{−1/5+u} for the four-corner set. Random variants have exact rates (arXiv:2609.33557). The gap between log n / n and n^{−1/5} remains. Open.",
 n="Fame: a well-known problem in geometric measure theory, posed explicitly by Nazarov, Peres and Volberg and the subject of a sustained line of work."),
dict(id='T182',
 x="Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Sarnak's Mobius disjointness conjecture: number theory and dynamics.",
 d='DYN',
 t="Sarnak's Möbius disjointness conjecture",
 s='Let μ be the Möbius function. Sarnak conjectured that for every continuous map T of a compact metric space X with zero topological entropy, every f ∈ C(X) and every x ∈ X, (1/N) Σ_{n ≤ N} f(T^n x) μ(n) → 0 as N → ∞.',
 c="The conjecture is implied by Chowla's conjecture on the vanishing of correlations of the Möbius (or Liouville) function, open since 1965. Tao showed that the logarithmically averaged versions of the two conjectures are equivalent. Zero entropy is necessary in general, since μ² itself is a point of a positive-entropy system. Compare T029 (Furstenberg's ×2 ×3 conjecture), a different rigidity question in the same area.",
 src="P. Sarnak, Three lectures on the Möbius function, randomness and dynamics (2010); status as in S. Ferenczi, J. Kułaga-Przymus and M. Lemańczyk, Sarnak's conjecture: what's new, in Ergodic Theory and Dynamical Systems in their Interactions with Arithmetics and Combinatorics, Lecture Notes in Math. 2213 (2018) 163–235",
 loc="Ferenczi–Kułaga-Przymus–Lemańczyk, arXiv:1710.04039, Introduction: 'In 2010, Sarnak [148, 149] formulated the following conjecture: (2) Each zero entropy continuous map T of a compact metric space X is Möbius disjoint.' and 'Chowla conjecture ⇒ Sarnak's conjecture'.",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.04039',
 why='The conjecture expresses the principle that the Möbius function does not correlate with any deterministic sequence, which is the form of randomness of the primes needed in many problems of analytic number theory. It has organised a large body of work joining ergodic theory, higher-order Fourier analysis and multiplicative number theory since 2010.',
 st="Möbius disjointness is known for many classes: rotations (Davenport), nilsystems (Green–Tao), horocycle flows (Bourgain–Sarnak–Ziegler), and various classes of interval exchange transformations and rank-one systems surveyed by Ferenczi, Kułaga-Przymus and Lemańczyk (arXiv:1710.04039). Matomäki and Radziwiłł's theorem on multiplicative functions in short intervals gave the averaged form of two-point Chowla (Matomäki–Radziwiłł–Tao); Tao proved the logarithmic two-point Chowla conjecture and the equivalence of logarithmic Chowla and logarithmic Sarnak; Tao and Teräväinen proved logarithmic Chowla for odd order; Frantzikinakis and Host proved the logarithmic Sarnak conjecture for zero-entropy systems with countably many ergodic invariant measures (Ann. of Math. 187 (2018) 869–931). The conjecture in its original Cesàro form remains open in general. Open.",
 n='Fame: one of the central conjectures linking dynamics and number theory.'),
dict(id='T183',
 x="Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Montgomery's pair correlation conjecture: number theory.",
 d='RMT',
 t="Montgomery's pair correlation conjecture for the zeros of the Riemann zeta function",
 s='Assume the Riemann hypothesis and write the nontrivial zeros as 1/2 + iγ. Montgomery conjectured that for fixed 0 < a < b, #{(γ, γ′): 0 < γ, γ′ ≤ T, 2πa/log T ≤ γ − γ′ ≤ 2πb/log T} ~ (T log T / 2π) ∫_a^b (1 − (sin πu/πu)²) du as T → ∞, equivalently that his function F(α) equals 1 for all |α| ≥ 1. This is the pair correlation of the eigenvalues of large random matrices from the Gaussian Unitary Ensemble.',
 c='Montgomery proved, under the Riemann hypothesis, that F(α) = |α| + T^{−2|α|} log T (1 + o(1)) for |α| ≤ 1, which determines the pair correlation against test functions with Fourier transform supported in (−1, 1); the conjecture is the extension to all α. Gallagher and Mueller showed that the conjecture implies that almost all zeros are simple. Compare T107 (the Fyodorov–Hiary–Keating conjecture), T089 (Bohigas–Giannoni–Schmit) and T105 (Berry–Tabor), which concern other random-matrix predictions; T184 concerns moments of zeta.',
 src='H. L. Montgomery, The pair correlation of zeros of the zeta function, in Analytic Number Theory, Proc. Sympos. Pure Math. 24, Amer. Math. Soc. (1973) 181–193',
 loc="Goldston–Lee–Schettler–Suriajaya, arXiv:2503.15449, abstract: 'Montgomery in 1973 introduced the Pair Correlation Conjecture (PCC) for zeros of the Riemann zeta-function. He also conjectured that asymptotically 100% of the zeros are simple.'",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.15449',
 why='The conjecture is the original link between the zeros of the zeta function and random matrix theory, and the agreement with GUE statistics found numerically by Odlyzko is one of the most striking pieces of evidence for the Hilbert–Pólya philosophy. It is equivalent, through Goldston and Montgomery, to an asymptotic for the variance of primes in short intervals.',
 st="Montgomery (1973) proved the conjecture for test functions with Fourier support in (−1, 1) under the Riemann hypothesis. Rudnick and Sarnak (Duke Math. J. 81 (1996)) proved the analogous result for all n-level correlations with restricted support; Goldston and Montgomery related the conjecture to primes in short intervals; Odlyzko's computations of zeros near the 10^20-th and later zeros agree closely with the GUE prediction. Goldston, Lee, Schettler and Suriajaya (arXiv:2503.15449) showed that the conjecture implies that almost all zeros are simple and on the critical line. No extension of the support beyond (−1, 1) is known. Open.",
 n='Fame: one of the best-known conjectures in analytic number theory and the founding conjecture of the random-matrix model of L-functions.'),
dict(id='T184',
 x='Moved on 12 Nov 2026 to the list of open problems in mathematics generally (06_math_research/top200_general_2026_11_10, seed_from_probability.tsv): Keating-Snaith moments conjecture: number theory.',
 d='RMT',
 t='The Keating–Snaith conjecture for the moments of the Riemann zeta function',
 s='For k > −1/2, Keating and Snaith conjectured that (1/T) ∫_0^T |ζ(1/2 + it)|^{2k} dt ~ a_k g_k/Γ(k² + 1) (log T)^{k²} as T → ∞, where a_k is the arithmetic factor Π_p (1 − 1/p)^{k²} Σ_{m≥0} (Γ(m + k)/(m! Γ(k)))² p^{−m} and g_k/Γ(k² + 1) = G(k + 1)²/G(2k + 1), with G the Barnes G-function. The constant is obtained from the moments of characteristic polynomials of random unitary matrices.',
 c='The cases k = 1 (Hardy–Littlewood) and k = 2 (Ingham) are classical; the conjectured constants for k = 3 (g_3 = 42, Conrey–Ghosh) and k = 4 (g_4 = 24024, Conrey–Gonek) predate the random-matrix derivation, which gave the constant for all k. The conjecture is open for every k other than 1 and 2, already for k = 3. The lower-order terms are predicted by the recipe of Conrey, Farmer, Keating, Rubinstein and Snaith. Compare T183 (pair correlation) and T107 (the maximum of zeta on short intervals).',
 src='J. P. Keating and N. C. Snaith, Random matrix theory and ζ(1/2 + it), Comm. Math. Phys. 214 (2000) 57–89',
 loc="Bui–Gonek–Milinovich, arXiv:1302.5032, abstract: 'Keating and Snaith modeled the Riemann zeta-function ζ(s) by characteristic polynomials of random NxN unitary matrices, and used this to conjecture the asymptotic main term for the 2k-th moment of ζ(1/2+it) when k>-1/2.'",
 url='https://arxiv.org/abs/1302.5032',
 why='The moments of the zeta function on the critical line are a classical object of analytic number theory, and the Keating–Snaith formula was the first derivation of the leading constant for all k, using a probabilistic model rather than number theory. It made the moments of characteristic polynomials of random matrices a standard tool and connected the problem to log-correlated fields and Gaussian multiplicative chaos.',
 st='The order of magnitude (log T)^{k²} is known in several ranges: Radziwiłł and Soundararajan, and Heap and Soundararajan, proved matching lower bounds for all k > 0; under the Riemann hypothesis Harper proved the sharp upper bound for all k > 0; unconditionally Heap, Radziwiłł and Soundararajan proved the sharp upper bound for 0 ≤ k ≤ 2 (Q. J. Math. 70 (2019) 1387–1396). The asymptotic with the conjectured constant is known only for k = 1 and k = 2 (with lower-order terms for k = 2). Moments of moments over short intervals were studied by Bailey and Keating (arXiv:2006.04503). Open.',
 n='Fame: a central conjecture of analytic number theory and the best-known application of random matrix theory to L-functions.'),
dict(id="T185", d="Q", t="Stability of α-fair bandwidth sharing networks with general file-size distributions",
 s="In the connection-level model of Massoulié and Roberts, flows on each route arrive as a Poisson (or renewal) process and bring a random amount of work with a general distribution, and the link capacities are shared among the flows present according to the weighted α-fair allocation. Prove that whenever the network is subcritical (the offered load on every link is below its capacity) the process describing the flows and their residual work is positive recurrent.",
 c="For exponential file sizes the number of flows per route is a Markov chain and stability under α-fair policies for all α > 0 is known (de Veciana–Lee–Konstantopoulos, Bonald–Massoulié). For general file sizes the state must include residual work, and the Markov-chain arguments for the exponential case do not transfer. A standard route is to prove stability of the fluid model and then deduce stability of the stochastic model; Fu and Williams treat the first step.",
 src="M. Bramson, Network stability under max–min fair bandwidth sharing, Ann. Appl. Probab. 20 (2010) 1126–1176; status as in Y. Fu and R. J. Williams, Stability of a subcritical fluid model for fair bandwidth sharing with general file size distributions, Stoch. Syst. 10 (2020) 251–273",
 loc="Fu–Williams, Stoch. Syst. 10 (2020) 251–273, doi:10.1287/stsy.2019.0058, abstract: 'Since the year 2000, it has been a standing problem to prove stability of the data communications network model of Massoulié and Roberts [...], with general file sizes and operating under fair bandwidth sharing policies, when the offered load is less than capacity (subcritical conditions). A crucial step in an approach to this problem is to prove stability of subcritical fluid model solutions.'",
 url="https://doi.org/10.1287/stsy.2019.0058",
 why="Bandwidth sharing networks are the standard flow-level model of Internet congestion control, and the α-fair family contains proportional fairness and TCP-like allocations. Stability under general file sizes is the basic sanity property of such a protocol, and it is a test case for the fluid-model method of proving positive recurrence in networks where the state includes residual service times.",
 st="Bramson (arXiv:1010.3142) proved stability of weighted max–min fair sharing for general interarrival and service distributions with 2 + δ moments. Massoulié (arXiv:0707.4542) proved stability of proportional fairness with Markovian routing and phase-type service times. Gromoll and Williams (Ann. Appl. Probab. 2009) established fluid limits. Paganini, Tang, Ferragut and Andrew (IEEE Trans. Automat. Control 57 (2012) 579–591) gave a Lyapunov function for the fluid model under smoothness assumptions, and Fu and Williams proved stability of subcritical fluid model solutions for general file sizes. Positive recurrence of the stochastic model for α-fair policies with general file sizes is the step these works leave open. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a recognised standing problem in stochastic networks since 2000; known mainly within applied probability and networking."),
dict(id="T186", d="RG", t="A scaling limit for the circumference of G(n, c/n) for every c > 1",
 s="Let L_n(c) be the length of the longest cycle (the circumference) of the Erdős–Rényi graph G(n, c/n). Prove that for every c > 1 there is a constant f(c) such that L_n(c)/n → f(c) in probability (or almost surely) as n → ∞.",
 c="The existence of cycles of linear length for every c > 1, conjectured by Erdős, was proved by Ajtai, Komlós and Szemerédi and independently by Fernandez de la Vega. Subadditivity arguments do not apply directly, so even the existence of the limit is nontrivial. In the weakly supercritical regime c = 1 + ε the best bounds are 1.581ε² ≤ ℓ_min(1 + ε) ≤ 1.7395ε² (Anastos; Kemkes–Wormald), so the asymptotic constant of the circumference near criticality is also unknown. For large c the longest path has the same first-order limit (Anastos–Frieze).",
 src="M. Anastos and A. Frieze, A scaling limit for the length of the longest cycle in a sparse random graph, J. Combin. Theory Ser. B 148 (2021) 184–208; status as in M. Anastos, J. Erde, M. Kang and V. Pfenninger, arXiv:2503.14336",
 loc="Anastos–Erde–Kang–Pfenninger, arXiv:2503.14336, Section 1: 'the bound on c in Theorem 1.1 was not made explicit, but parts of the arguments require that c ≥ 10^6. However, the result was later shown to hold for all c ≥ 20 by the first author [3, Theorem 1].' Section 8: 'whilst the distribution of the circumference of a sparse random graph G(n, c/n) is well understood now when c is large, there is still a large gap in our knowledge for smaller c, where even a scaling limit has not yet been established.'",
 url="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.14336",
 why="The circumference is one of the basic global parameters of the sparse random graph, and the problem goes back to Erdős's question about long cycles. Unlike the giant component or the k-core, it has no known local description for small c, so a scaling limit would require new structural understanding of the giant component just above criticality.",
 st="Ajtai, Komlós and Szemerédi proved L_n(c) ≥ ℓ(c)n w.h.p. for every c > 1. Frieze determined 1 − ℓ_min(c) up to a factor 1 + o_c(1) as c → ∞. Anastos and Frieze (arXiv:1907.03657) proved that L_n(c)/n → f(c) almost surely for sufficiently large c, with f computable to any precision; Anastos (arXiv:2105.13828) extended this to c ≥ 20; Anastos, Erde, Kang and Pfenninger (arXiv:2503.14336) proved a central limit theorem for c ≥ 20. Below c = 20, and in particular below the threshold for the strong 4-core (numerically c* ≈ 9.2), no scaling limit is known. Open.",
 n="Fame borderline (my judgment): a natural open question about the most studied random graph model, stated by the authors working on it; known to random-graph specialists."),
]
